# Usage

The examples below describe the basic usage of the Checkstyle Plugin.

Generate Checkstyle Report As Part of the Project Reports
---------------------------------------------------------

Generate the Checkstyle report as part of the Project Reports.
Add the Checkstyle Plugin to the `<reporting>` section of your `pom.xml`.

```xml
<project>
  ...
   <reporting>
      <plugins>
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-checkstyle-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${project.version}</version>
          <reportSets>
            <reportSet>
              <reports>
                <report>checkstyle</report>
              </reports>
            </reportSet>
          </reportSets>
        </plugin>
      </plugins>
    </reporting>
  ...
</project>
```

Run the site phase to generate the report.

```shell
mvn site
```

Generate Checkstyle Report As Standalone
----------------------------------------

You can also generate the Checkstyle report with the `checkstyle:checkstyle` goal from the command line.
You do not need to specify the Checkstyle Plugin in your `pom.xml` unless you need a specific configuration.

```shell
mvn checkstyle:checkstyle
```

Check for Violations as Part of the Build
-----------------------------------------

If you want the console report or a build failure, add an execution of `checkstyle::check` to the `<build>` element.
Configure any options that you need.

Options that you set in the `<reporting>` element do not affect executions in the `<build>` element.

The phase that `checkstyle::check` is bound to is important.
If you bind it to the validate phase, it checks the code before compilation.
It is guaranteed to run.
If the code is invalid, the errors that checkstyle reports can differ from the errors from the javac compiler.

Another common option binds the goal to the verify phase.
The verify phase runs much later than the validate phase.
A later phase lets the javac compiler flag invalid code before checkstyle runs.

If developers run `mvn test` before they push changes, checkstyle does not run.
The verify phase occurs after the test phase.

For example:

```xml
 <plugin>
   <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
   <artifactId>maven-checkstyle-plugin</artifactId>
   <version>${project.version}</version>
   <configuration>
     <configLocation>checkstyle.xml</configLocation>
     <inputEncoding>UTF-8</inputEncoding>
     <consoleOutput>true</consoleOutput>
     <failsOnError>true</failsOnError>
     <linkXRef>false</linkXRef>
   </configuration>
   <executions>
     <execution>
       <id>validate</id>
       <phase>validate</phase>
       <goals>
         <goal>check</goal>
       </goals>
     </execution>
   </executions>
 </plugin>
```
